#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-install-issues: the open issues that hold a task or concern a
check class on an install that was missing, read at the start of a run for
whether the tool stands on this machine now, and closed by devloop where it
does.

Since 28 October 2026, decision (5) of the roadmap entry of 27 October 2026
on the tool state read by one program, built in the roadmap entry of 28
October 2026 on the hook on SubagentStart. A handover under a no that the
person declines leaves an issue carrying the command, which blocks the task;
until that day the task went on only once they had run the command and
closed the issue themselves, build-work step 3 point 7 and "The task
itself", and the issues a refusal of auto mode leaves, and the one
setup-checks writes at secrets with nobody there, asked the same of them.
The person's wording of 27 October 2026 says instead: "Installierst du es
spaeter doch, merke ich das beim naechsten Start, schliesse das Issue und
baue die Aufgabe." So at the next start devloop reads, for every such issue,
whether the tool stands on this machine now, and closes the issue itself
where it does, with a comment naming the line it read; a task the issue
held is free again, which the readiness query of build-work then finds, and
a check class the issue concerned goes to setup-checks from the next task,
as build-work says for the issues after a refusal of auto mode. Read is
whether the tool stands for use - on the search path for a command, as
bin/devloop-tool-state --build reads it, in the vendor's folder for a
browser for tests - and not whether the handed-over command installed: the
person may have installed by another way, and a tool the search path finds
is one the work can use, "A command handed over is backed, and its result
is read" in docs/skill-conventions.md. The decision as recorded named
--installed; the entry of 28 October 2026 on the hook on SubagentStart says
why the search path is read. Beside it, since the entry of 28 October 2026
on the handover steps, where the search path does not reach a command's
tool, the folder the issue's command installs into is read with
bin/devloop-tool-state --installed, the command and the tool of the issue:
go install puts a tool into ~/go/bin, which often stands on no search path,
and the person then has installed as the issue asked and gets told nothing
of why the issue stays open. Where that folder line says found, this
program prints it as a line of its own, and the run says that the command
installed the tool into the folder, that the folder is not in their PATH,
that the issue therefore stays open, and the steps that put the folder on
their search path, shared/install-issues-read.md, the wording (11) of that
entry. The issue stays open in that case: the search path alone says
whether the work can use the tool. The person's sentence of 27 October
2026 above stands as the record of that day; since 28 October 2026 the
handover says instead that where they install the tool later, devloop
closes the issue at the next start and builds the task, wording (1) of the
entry on the handover steps.

Usage
    devloop-install-issues [DIR]
    devloop-install-issues --after-install [DIR]
    devloop-install-issues --self-test

DIR is the project directory; without it CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, and without
that the current directory, as bin/devloop-install-record takes it. The
skills run it at every start, before the first thing that reads an open
issue: the readiness query of build-work step 2, and the question on the
rule for the classifier of auto mode in build-work and in plan-work. And
since 30 October 2026, with --after-install, inside a session: where the
person, after their no to the handover of a task's tool, typed the command
in Claude Code with ! in the same session, or said that they ran it in a
second terminal window, build-work step 3 point 7 and "The task itself",
the run reads the result with bin/devloop-tool-state --installed and then
runs this program, which closes the issue as at a start where the search
path finds the tool; the one difference is the comment, which then says
that the closing followed an install typed in the session and names no
start. Decided by the person on 30 October 2026, the roadmap entry of that
date on the install: the run of 29 October 2026 closed the issue in the
session through this program, whose comment named a start that did not
happen. The comment is text for the tracker and has no approved wording.

Which issues. The open issues of the project labelled raised-here and
needs-human, read with gh issue list, whose body carries the two lines every
issue devloop writes for a missing install carries, shared/install-issue.md,
each at the start of a line of its own:
    install-tool: TOOL
    install-command: COMMAND
TOOL as bin/devloop-tool-state names it - a command as it is called, yq, or
a browser for tests as VENDOR:NAME, playwright:webkit - and COMMAND the
install command as it was handed over, alone, without the change into the
project's directory. An issue without both lines is no issue of this kind
and is passed over; the first line of each name counts where there are
several; the value is read without the spaces around it and without a
carriage return. The kind of each issue: it holds a task where an open
issue is blocked by it, read with gh api
repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/N/dependencies/blocking, the relation the
readiness query reads from the other side, as bin/devloop-auto-mode-question
reads it; it concerns a check class otherwise. gh is found on PATH through
shutil.which, as that program finds it, and called in DIR, each call with 60
seconds, or with what is left of the ninety-five seconds of the whole call
where that is less, below.

How a command that does not answer is ended, since 14 November 2026, the
roadmap entry of that date on gh under the bound. Every command this
program runs - gh, and bash with bin/devloop-tool-state - runs under
bin/devloop-bounded, the program beside this one, as
bin/devloop-auto-mode-question runs gh since that day and for the reasons
its head gives: a command that has not ended at its limit is ended with
everything it started, its process group sent SIGTERM and, where something
of it stands a second later, SIGKILL, the bound back at most two seconds
after the limit with exit 124 and its line, read here as no answer within
the seconds of that limit; the output goes to two files and through no
pipe, and the input is empty, so that the call is back with the bound on
every system; what the bound does not reach its head says, the block of
milestone 12 on Windows with Git Bash in docs/defects.md among it. The
limits of a command: gh 60 seconds a call. bin/devloop-tool-state bounds its
own queries with sixty seconds each and holds, since 15 November 2026,
ninety-five over its whole call, a query that does not answer leaving its
line not read there, and this program's limit for it lies above what that
takes: 72 seconds under --build, which with one TOOL asks at most one
query, sixty seconds and the two its bound may take to return, and ten for
the program itself; 107 seconds under --installed, the ninety-five of that
program's whole call, the two and the ten, where it was 134 until that day,
124 seconds for the two queries of go and the ten. Over them stands the
limit of this program's own call, the next paragraph: a command has its own
limit, or what is left of the call's ninety-five seconds where that is
less, which under --installed it always is. So that program's own bound
ends a query before this program's ends that program wherever that much of
the call is left, and it prints its line not read; where less is left,
this program's bound may end it first, and the answer is not decided with
the seconds that were left. Where this program's bound fires, the bound of
that program, which stands in the group this one's bound ends, ends its own
command first, bin/devloop-bounded on a bound inside a bound.

The limit over the whole call, since 15 November 2026, the roadmap entry of
that date on a limit over the whole call. The program holds ninety-five
seconds over its call, counted from its start, as bin/devloop-arm and
bin/devloop-start-conditions hold theirs: no command starts once fewer than
ten of them are left, and one that starts has its own limit, or what is
left where that is less. So the program ends inside the 120 seconds of a
Bash call, the limit of the call the texts start it in, however many issues
it reads and however slowly each command answers: the last command is back
at most two seconds after the ninety-five, the two its bound may take. A
command that is not started answers as one that did not answer: issues: not
decided, with the command and the message "no time left within the 95
seconds of this call", the words of bin/devloop-arm, after the groups read
until then; an issue closed before that stays closed, and the next start
reads the others again. Until that day every command had its own limit and
the whole call none, the list and up to four commands for every issue, a
hundred issues at most: measured on 15 November 2026 at 6f92a5d on macOS
and in alpine:3.22 with a stand-in for gh whose list answers after 50
seconds, whose first blocking relation after 55 and whose second not at
all, the program was back after 167 and 165 seconds.

Until 14 November 2026 every command ran through
subprocess.run with a timeout of sixty seconds, which ends the one process
it started: what the command had started ran on, under
bin/devloop-tool-state its bound with the query, until that bound's own
sixty seconds; and the two limits being the same number, this program's
fired first and ended with issues: not decided where the tool state program
would have printed its line a moment later. Measured on 14 November 2026 on
macOS and in alpine:3.22 with a stand-in for the project's Playwright that
starts a sleep and waits in a second one: back after 60.011 and 60.025
seconds with no answer within 60 seconds and exit 2, and, this program's
limit set to one second, the bound of the tool state program and both
sleeps standing for the rest of its sixty.

What is read. For each issue, bin/devloop-tool-state --build TOOL, the
program beside this one run with bash in DIR, whose first line is the
tool's: found, not found, or not read. Found: the issue is closed with gh
issue close N --comment, the comment naming the line:
    devloop closed this issue at the start of a session, the tool standing on this machine now: <the line>
or, with --after-install:
    devloop closed this issue after an install typed in the session, the tool standing on this machine now: <the line>
Not found, or not read: nothing is done, and the issue stays open with the
task it holds. A TOOL the program refuses - not a bare word and not
VENDOR:NAME of one of its vendors, which the program alone judges, so that
this one carries no second reading of the form - is read as not read, with
the program's message, and the issue stays: what devloop cannot read it
does not close.

Output, one group per issue in the order of the numbers, each line one key:
    issue: N
    kind: task|class
    title: <the title>
    tool: <the line of bin/devloop-tool-state, or not read, because ...>
    folder: <the first line of bin/devloop-tool-state --installed COMMAND TOOL, where it says found>
    command: COMMAND
    closed: yes|no
The folder line stands only where the tool line says not found on the
search path and the folder line of --installed says found at: the command
installed and the search path does not reach the tool. Where --installed
says not found or not read, or refuses the command's installer, no folder
line stands, there being nothing to tell. Six lines per group, seven with
the folder line. And, where no open issue carries the two lines, one line:
    issues: none of the N open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries the lines install-tool and install-command
Exit 0 where every such issue was read, closed or not. Exit 2 where a query
did not answer - gh issue list, the read of the blocking relation, the tool
state program under --build or --installed, or the closing - with the
groups read until then, then
    issues: not decided
    command: <the command that did not answer>
    message: <what came back>
so that the run says so, as "When a command does not answer" asks, and
concludes nothing of the issues not read; an issue closed before that stays
closed, which the comment on it says. Exit 2 with one line on stderr on an
argument it does not know.

It needs python3, bash, git and gh, and nothing else. gh, and bash for the
call of bin/devloop-tool-state, are looked up on PATH on every system, and
where PATH holds none, that command is not started and the message is the
system's for a file that is not there, naming it, since 14 November 2026.
On macOS and Linux bash is looked up for that call alone, the bound running
by its first line, /bin/bash, so that with no bash on PATH the calls of gh
before it run all the same. Under Windows bash runs the bound of every
command, and where PATH holds none, no command is started and the message
names bash, since 15 November 2026; from 14 November 2026 until then the
bare name bash went into the command line there, every call of gh was
started all the same, and the message was [WinError 2] with the text of
Windows, naming nothing, as the head of bin/devloop-auto-mode-question says
with the lines of CPython; and until 15 November 2026 this head said of
every system that nothing is started where PATH holds no gh or no bash.
The self-test runs
every outcome against a stand-in for gh that answers from scripted files, as
the self-test of bin/devloop-auto-mode-question does - the stand-in first on
the path, a launcher of sh on macOS and Linux and of cmd on Windows, the
configuration of gh pointing at an empty directory, no token in the
environment - and against the real bin/devloop-tool-state beside this
program, with a tool that stands on the self-test's own path and one that
does not, one installed by a stand-in for go into a folder the search path
does not reach, and a browser in a project without its package, which the
program cannot read; nothing of it reaches GitHub, and the settings of this
machine are not touched. The cases of a command that does not answer, since
14 November 2026: the stand-in for gh, a stand-in for the project's
Playwright under --build and the stand-in for go under --installed each
start a second process and stand with it, each writing a mark into a file
of its own, for a minute at most; the self-test loads this program in an
interpreter of its own, sets the limit of that call to a few seconds there
and calls it, so that no setting of the environment moves a limit in
operation, and holds the answer to the message with those seconds, to the
files standing still once the program has returned, and to the call being
back no later than those seconds and two after a call that is answered at
once. The cases on the limit over the whole call, since 15 November 2026:
where DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK names a file, the self-test's loader puts that
file in the place of the program's clock, the seconds since its start, and
the stand-in for gh adds to it the seconds a case gives an answer, as the
stand-in of bin/devloop-arm does, so that the cases wait for nothing; the
loader alone reads that variable, and no setting of the environment moves
the clock in operation. A call of gh and a call of the tool state program
are not started with nine of the ninety-five seconds left, and every
command of an issue is started with ten; and the tool state program,
started with a few seconds left, the fewest a command starts with set to
those few by the loader, has those and not its 72. And no bash on PATH:
under Windows no command started and the message naming bash; on macOS and
Linux gh called and the tool state program not started, the message naming
bash.
"""

import errno
import json
import os
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
TOOL_STATE = os.path.join(HERE, "devloop-tool-state")
BOUNDED = os.path.join(HERE, "devloop-bounded")
LABELS = ["raised-here", "needs-human"]
SECONDS = 60
# the limits for bin/devloop-tool-state, above what that program takes: under --build one query, sixty seconds and the two its bound may take to return, and ten for the program itself; under --installed the ninety-five seconds of that program's whole call, the two and the ten, more than this program's own call ever has left
BUILD_SECONDS = 72
INSTALLED_SECONDS = 107
# the limit over the whole call and the fewest seconds of it a command starts with, as bin/devloop-arm holds them, since 15 November 2026
LIMIT = 95
STEP = 10
BEGAN = time.monotonic()


def now():
    """The seconds since the program began; the self-test's loader puts a
    clock of its own here."""
    return time.monotonic() - BEGAN


def usage(msg):
    print("devloop-install-issues: %s" % msg, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def project_dir(given):
    return given or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd()


def one_line(s):
    return " ".join(s.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n").split("\n"))


def quoted(word):
    """A word of a Windows command line in double quotes, as
    subprocess.list2cmdline writes a word that holds a blank: a quote behind
    a backslash, and the backslashes before a quote or before the end of the
    word doubled."""
    out = []
    slashes = 0
    for c in word:
        if c == "\\":
            slashes += 1
            continue
        out.append("\\" * (slashes * 2 + 1) + c if c == '"' else "\\" * slashes + c)
        slashes = 0
    return '"' + "".join(out) + "\\" * (slashes * 2) + '"'


def under_bound(seconds, words):
    """The call that runs the words of a command under bin/devloop-bounded
    with the seconds: on macOS and Linux the bound by its first line, a
    list; on Windows one command line, bash found on PATH running the bound,
    every word in double quotes and the command's own path with forward
    slashes, a word bash reads as a path; the head says why. None under
    Windows where PATH holds no bash: the caller then starts nothing."""
    if os.name != "nt":
        return [BOUNDED, str(seconds)] + words
    bash = shutil.which("bash")
    if bash is None:  # under Windows no bash on PATH to run the bound
        return None
    return " ".join(quoted(w) for w in [bash, BOUNDED, str(seconds), words[0].replace("\\", "/")] + words[1:])


def run(args, d, env=None, seconds=None):
    """The output of a command, or None with the command shown and the
    message. The first word is found on PATH through shutil.which, as a
    shell finds it; where PATH holds none, nothing is started and the
    message names it. The command runs under bin/devloop-bounded with the
    seconds, SECONDS where none are given, or with what is left of the LIMIT
    of the whole call where that is less, its output into two files and its
    input empty: one that does not answer is ended by the bound with
    everything it started, exit 124 and the bound's line on stderr, read
    here as no answer within the seconds it had. Where fewer than STEP
    seconds of LIMIT are left the command is not started and answers as one
    that did not answer, in the words of bin/devloop-arm; and under
    Windows, where PATH holds no bash to run the bound, it is not started
    and the message names bash."""
    shown = " ".join(args)
    if seconds is None:
        seconds = SECONDS
    exe = shutil.which(args[0])
    if exe is None:  # the first word not on PATH: nothing started, the system's message for a file that is not there
        return None, shown, str(FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), args[0])), None
    left = int(LIMIT - now())
    if left < STEP:  # fewer than STEP seconds of the whole call left: not started, answered as a command that did not answer
        return None, shown, "no time left within the %d seconds of this call" % LIMIT, None
    seconds = min(seconds, left)  # the command's own limit, or what is left of the whole call's where that is less
    call = under_bound(seconds, [exe] + args[1:])
    if call is None:  # under Windows no bash on PATH: nothing started, the message as for the first word, naming bash
        return None, shown, str(FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "bash")), None
    try:
        with tempfile.TemporaryFile(mode="w+") as out, tempfile.TemporaryFile(mode="w+") as err:
            rc = subprocess.run(call, cwd=d, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                stdout=out, stderr=err, env=env).returncode
            out.seek(0)
            err.seek(0)
            r = subprocess.CompletedProcess(args, rc, out.read(), err.read())
    except OSError as e:
        return None, shown, str(e), None
    if r.returncode == 124 and any(line.startswith("no answer within %d seconds: " % seconds) for line in r.stderr.split("\n")):
        return None, shown, "no answer within %d seconds" % seconds, None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, shown, (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip() or
                             "exit %d with nothing printed" % r.returncode), r
    return r.stdout, shown, None, r


def field(body, name):
    """The value of the first line of the body that starts with name and a
    colon, without the spaces around it; None where there is none."""
    for line in body.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n").split("\n"):
        if line.startswith(name + ":"):
            return line[len(name) + 1:].strip()
    return None


def not_decided(command, message):
    print("issues: not decided")
    print("command: %s" % one_line(command))
    print("message: %s" % one_line(message))
    return 2


def tool_line(tool, d):
    """The first line of bin/devloop-tool-state --build TOOL, and whether
    it says found; (line, found, failure) with failure the command and the
    message where the program did not answer."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = d
    out, shown, err, r = run(["bash", TOOL_STATE, "--build", tool], d, env, BUILD_SECONDS)
    shown = "bin/devloop-tool-state --build %s" % tool
    if r is None:
        return None, False, (shown, err)
    if r.returncode == 2:
        return "not read, because bin/devloop-tool-state refused the tool: %s" % one_line(r.stderr.strip() or err or ""), False, None
    if r.returncode not in (0, 1):
        return None, False, (shown, err or "ended with %d" % r.returncode)
    first = (r.stdout.split("\n") or [""])[0].strip()
    if not first.startswith(tool + ": "):
        return None, False, (shown, "printed no line for %s: %s" % (tool, one_line(r.stdout.strip() or r.stderr.strip())))
    return first, first.startswith(tool + ": found at "), None


def folder_line(tool, command, d):
    """The first line of bin/devloop-tool-state --installed COMMAND TOOL,
    the folder the command's installer writes to, read where the search
    path does not reach the tool, since the entry of 28 October 2026 on the
    handover steps: (line, failure), the line only where it says found at,
    None where it says not found or not read or the program refuses the
    command or the tool, and failure the command and the message where the
    program did not answer."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = d
    out, shown, err, rr = run(["bash", TOOL_STATE, "--installed", command, tool], d, env, INSTALLED_SECONDS)
    shown = "bin/devloop-tool-state --installed '%s' %s" % (command, tool)
    if rr is None:  # the folder read: the program did not answer
        return None, (shown, err)
    if rr.returncode == 2:  # the folder read: the command's installer or the tool refused, nothing to tell
        return None, None
    if rr.returncode not in (0, 1):  # the folder read: ended otherwise than the program ends
        return None, (shown, err or "ended with %d" % rr.returncode)
    folder = (rr.stdout.split("\n") or [""])[0].strip()
    if folder.startswith(tool + ": found at "):  # the folder read: the command installed there
        return folder, None
    return None, None


def read(d, after_install=False):
    out, shown, err, _ = run(["gh", "issue", "list", "--state", "open",
                              "--label", LABELS[0], "--label", LABELS[1],
                              "--limit", "100", "--json", "number,title,body"], d)
    if out is None:
        return not_decided(shown, err)
    try:
        issues = json.loads(out)
        if not isinstance(issues, list):
            raise ValueError("not a list")
    except ValueError as e:
        return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
    found = []
    for issue in sorted((i for i in issues if isinstance(i, dict)),
                        key=lambda i: i.get("number") if isinstance(i.get("number"), int) else 0):
        number = issue.get("number")
        if not isinstance(number, int):
            continue
        body = issue.get("body") or ""
        tool = field(body, "install-tool")
        command = field(body, "install-command")
        if not tool or not command:
            continue
        found.append((number, issue.get("title") or "", tool, command))
    if not found:
        print("issues: none of the %d open issues labelled %s and %s carries the lines "
              "install-tool and install-command" % (len(issues), LABELS[0], LABELS[1]))
        return 0
    for number, title, tool, command in found:
        out, shown, err, _ = run(["gh", "api", "repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/%d/dependencies/blocking" % number], d)
        if out is None:
            return not_decided(shown, err)
        try:
            blocking = json.loads(out)
            if not isinstance(blocking, list):
                raise ValueError("not a list")
        except ValueError as e:
            return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
        kind = "task" if any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("state") == "open"
                             for b in blocking) else "class"
        line, stands, failure = tool_line(tool, d)
        if failure is not None:
            return not_decided(*failure)
        folder = None
        if not stands and line.startswith(tool + ": not found on the search path"):
            folder, failure = folder_line(tool, command, d)
            if failure:  # the folder read did not answer
                return not_decided(*failure)
        print("issue: %d" % number)
        print("kind: %s" % kind)
        print("title: %s" % one_line(title))
        print("tool: %s" % line)
        if folder is not None:
            print("folder: %s" % folder)
        print("command: %s" % one_line(command))
        if not stands:
            print("closed: no")
            continue
        # the comment names when devloop closed: at a start, or after an install the person typed in the session, --after-install, since 30 October 2026
        comment = ("devloop closed this issue %s, the tool standing on this machine now: %s"
                   % ("after an install typed in the session" if after_install else "at the start of a session", line))
        out, shown, err, _ = run(["gh", "issue", "close", str(number), "--comment", comment], d)
        if out is None:
            print("closed: no")
            return not_decided(shown, err)
        print("closed: yes")
    return 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the self-test

# The stand-in for gh: a Python program behind the launcher the system runs
# off PATH, as in the self-test of bin/devloop-auto-mode-question. It answers
# the issue list, the blocking relation of one issue and the closing of one
# issue from scripted files under $DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT, logs every call,
# and marks a call no answer was scripted for. Where a file .hang stands
# beside the scripted answer it does not answer: it starts a second process
# and stands with it, each writing a mark into a file of its own twenty
# times a second, thirty seconds at most, so that the self-test reads off
# the two files whether either still stands. Where a file .t stands beside
# the scripted answer it adds the seconds in it to the self-test's clock, the
# file $DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK names, before it answers, as the stand-in for gh
# of bin/devloop-arm does.
STAND_IN = r'''import os
import re
import sys
S = os.environ["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT"]
args = " ".join(sys.argv[1:])
with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
    f.write(args + "\n")
m = re.match(r"api repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/issues/([0-9]+)/dependencies/blocking$", args)
c = re.match(r"issue close ([0-9]+) --comment ", args)
if args.startswith("issue list "):
    a = os.path.join(S, "issues")
elif m:
    a = os.path.join(S, "blocking." + m.group(1))
elif c:
    a = os.path.join(S, "close." + c.group(1))
else:
    a = os.path.join(S, "other")
if os.path.exists(a + ".hang"):
    import subprocess
    import time
    marks = "import sys, time\nfor i in range(600):\n    open(sys.argv[1], 'a').write('.')\n    time.sleep(0.05)\n"
    subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, "-c", marks, os.path.join(S, "marks.started")])
    for i in range(600):
        open(os.path.join(S, "marks.gh"), "a").write(".")
        time.sleep(0.05)
    sys.exit(3)
if not os.path.exists(a + ".rc"):
    sys.stderr.write("stand-in for gh: no answer scripted for: gh %s\n" % args)
    open(os.path.join(S, "unscripted"), "w").close()
    sys.exit(1)
if os.path.exists(a + ".t"):
    clock = os.environ["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK"]
    told = float(open(clock, encoding="utf-8").read()) + float(open(a + ".t", encoding="utf-8").read())
    open(clock, "w", encoding="utf-8").write("%s\n" % told)
sys.stdout.write(open(a + ".out", encoding="utf-8").read())
sys.stderr.write(open(a + ".err", encoding="utf-8").read())
sys.exit(int(open(a + ".rc", encoding="utf-8").read().strip()))
'''
LAUNCHER_SH = '#!/bin/sh\nexec "%s" "%s" "$@"\n'
LAUNCHER_CMD = '@"%s" "%%~dp0gh-stand-in.py" %%*\r\n'
# The opening of a stand-in of sh, for go and for the project's Playwright,
# which bin/devloop-tool-state calls from bash: where the file named .hang
# stands under $DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT it does not answer, starts a second
# process and stands with it, each writing a mark into a file of its own, six
# hundred marks at most; without that file the stand-in goes on to its answer.
HANG_SH = """if [ -e "$DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT/%s.hang" ]; then
  ( i=0; while [ $i -lt 600 ]; do echo . >> "$DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT/marks.started"; sleep 0.05; i=$((i+1)); done ) &
  i=0; while [ $i -lt 600 ]; do echo . >> "$DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT/marks.%s"; sleep 0.05; i=$((i+1)); done
  exit 3
fi
"""
# The program loaded in an interpreter of its own, for the cases of the
# self-test that need what no call from outside sets: the limits named in the
# second argument, SECONDS=3 or BUILD_SECONDS=3, several joined by a comma,
# a command taken off the lookup where the third argument names it, gh or
# bash, and, where DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK names a file, the seconds in that
# file in the place of the program's clock. No setting of the environment
# moves a limit or the clock in operation: the loader alone reads that
# variable.
LOADER = """import os
import shutil
import sys
p, limits, gone = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]
g = {"__name__": "devloop_install_issues", "__file__": p}
exec(compile(open(p, encoding="utf-8").read(), p, "exec"), g)
for limit in limits.split(","):
    g[limit.split("=")[0]] = int(limit.split("=")[1])
clock = os.environ.get("DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK")
if clock:  # the self-test's clock in the place of the program's
    g["now"] = lambda: float(open(clock, encoding="utf-8").read())
found = shutil.which
shutil.which = lambda name, *a, **k: None if name == gone else found(name, *a, **k)
sys.exit(g["main"](sys.argv[4:]))
"""


def same(a, b):
    return os.path.normcase(os.path.realpath(a)) == os.path.normcase(os.path.realpath(b))


def selftest():
    me = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    T = os.path.realpath(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-install-issues."))
    n = [0]
    red = [0]
    try:
        for sub in ("bin", "gh-config", "config", "s", "p", "home", os.path.join("home", "bin")):
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, sub))
        stand_in = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh-stand-in.py")
        with open(stand_in, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(STAND_IN)
        if os.name == "nt":
            gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh.cmd")
            with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as f:
                f.write(LAUNCHER_CMD % sys.executable)
        else:
            gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh")
            with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write(LAUNCHER_SH % (sys.executable, stand_in))
            os.chmod(gh_path, os.stat(gh_path).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        # the tool that stands on the self-test's own path, and no other
        present = os.path.join(T, "home", "bin", "devloop-selftest-present")
        open(present, "w").close()
        os.chmod(present, os.stat(present).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        # the stand-in for go, answering go env GOBIN with a folder the search path does not reach, and the tool standing in that folder: an sh script, since bin/devloop-tool-state calls go from bash, on Windows under Git Bash as on the others
        go_bin = os.path.join(T, "home", "go", "bin")
        os.makedirs(go_bin)
        infolder = os.path.join(go_bin, "devloop-selftest-infolder")
        open(infolder, "w").close()
        os.chmod(infolder, os.stat(infolder).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        go_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "go")
        with open(go_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("#!/bin/sh\n" + HANG_SH % ("go", "go") + "printf '%s\\n' '%s'\n" % ("%s", go_bin.replace("\\", "/")))
        os.chmod(go_path, os.stat(go_path).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", os.path.join(T, "p")], check=True)
        env = dict(os.environ)
        for k in ("GH_TOKEN", "GITHUB_TOKEN", "GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN",
                  "GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN", "GH_HOST", "CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"):
            env.pop(k, None)
        env["PATH"] = os.path.join(T, "bin") + os.pathsep + os.path.join(T, "home", "bin") + os.pathsep + env.get("PATH", "")
        env["GH_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "gh-config")
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "config")
        env["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT"] = os.path.join(T, "s")
        env["HOME"] = os.path.join(T, "home")
        found = shutil.which("gh", path=env["PATH"])
        if found is None or not same(found, gh_path):
            print("FAIL the stand-in for gh is not the gh the path finds: %s" % found)
            return 1
        S = os.path.join(T, "s")

        def fresh():
            for name in os.listdir(S):
                os.unlink(os.path.join(S, name))
            open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "w").close()

        def ans(kind, rc, out, err=""):
            for suffix, content in ((".rc", "%d\n" % rc), (".out", out), (".err", err)):
                with open(os.path.join(S, kind + suffix), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write(content)

        def issues(*items):
            ans("issues", 0, json.dumps([{"number": num, "title": title, "body": body}
                                         for num, title, body in items]))

        def blocking(num, *states):
            ans("blocking.%d" % num, 0, json.dumps(
                [{"number": 900 + i, "state": s} for i, s in enumerate(states)]))

        def close(num, rc=0, err=""):
            ans("close.%d" % num, rc, "", err)

        last = {"out": ""}

        def go(*args):
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me] + list(args), cwd=os.path.join(T, "p"),
                               capture_output=True, text=True, env=env)
            last["out"] = r.stdout + r.stderr
            return r.returncode, last["out"]

        def got_out(_):
            return last["out"].split("\n")

        def loaded(limits, gone, *args, clock=None):
            """the program through LOADER: (exit code, output) and the
            seconds the call took; with clock, the program on the
            self-test's clock, a file that starts at those seconds"""
            e = dict(env)
            if clock is not None:  # the self-test's clock, which the stand-in for gh adds to
                e["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK"] = os.path.join(S, "clock")
                with open(e["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_CLOCK"], "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write("%d\n" % clock)
            began = time.monotonic()
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-c", LOADER, me, limits, gone] + list(args),
                               cwd=os.path.join(T, "p"), capture_output=True, text=True, env=e)
            last["out"] = r.stdout + r.stderr
            return (r.returncode, last["out"]), time.monotonic() - began

        def takes(kind, seconds):
            """the scripted answer of that kind takes the seconds on the self-test's clock"""
            with open(os.path.join(S, kind + ".t"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write("%d\n" % seconds)

        def standing_still(name, *files):
            """every file of marks there, so that its writer had started, and
            none longer half a second later, so that no writer stands"""
            n[0] += 1
            paths = [os.path.join(S, f) for f in files]
            before = [os.path.getsize(q) if os.path.exists(q) else -1 for q in paths]
            time.sleep(0.5)
            after = [os.path.getsize(q) if os.path.exists(q) else -1 for q in paths]
            for f, b, a in zip(files, before, after):
                if b < 1:
                    print("FAIL %s: %s was never written, its writer had not started" % (name, f)); red[0] += 1
                    return False
                if a != b:
                    print("FAIL %s: %s grew from %d to %d marks in half a second, its writer still stands" % (name, f, b, a)); red[0] += 1
                    return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def in_time(name, hung, at_once, limit):
            """the call that was not answered back no later than the limit and two seconds after the call that was answered at once"""
            n[0] += 1
            if hung > at_once + limit + 2:
                print("FAIL %s: back after %.3f seconds, %.3f where it is answered at once, more than the %d seconds and 2 apart" % (name, hung, at_once, limit)); red[0] += 1
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def calls():
            with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
                return [l.rstrip("\n") for l in f if l.strip()]

        def expect(name, got, want_rc, *want_lines, absent=()):
            rc, out = got
            n[0] += 1
            lines = out.split("\n")
            ok = True
            if rc != want_rc:
                print("FAIL %s: exit %d, expected %d" % (name, rc, want_rc))
                ok = False
            for w in want_lines:
                if ok and w not in lines:
                    print("FAIL %s: no line %r" % (name, w))
                    ok = False
            for w in absent:
                if ok and w in lines:
                    print("FAIL %s: the line %r stands there and should not" % (name, w))
                    ok = False
            if ok and os.path.exists(os.path.join(S, "unscripted")):
                print("FAIL %s: the stand-in was called for something no case scripted" % name)
                ok = False
            if not ok:
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                red[0] += 1
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def called(name, *want):
            n[0] += 1
            got = calls()
            if got != list(want):
                print("FAIL %s: calls %r, expected %r" % (name, got, list(want)))
                red[0] += 1
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        body_present = "The tool is missing.\n\ninstall-tool: devloop-selftest-present\ninstall-command: brew install devloop-selftest-present\n"
        body_absent = "install-tool: devloop-selftest-absent\r\ninstall-command:   brew install devloop-selftest-absent  \r\n"
        comment = "devloop closed this issue at the start of a session, the tool standing on this machine now: "
        present_line = "devloop-selftest-present: found at ~/bin/devloop-selftest-present (on %s)" % __import__("datetime").date.today().isoformat()

        # the arguments
        fresh()
        expect("an option it does not know: 2, one line on stderr", go("--x"), 2,
               "devloop-install-issues: unknown option --x; the options are --after-install and --self-test")
        fresh()
        expect("two directories: 2", go("a", "b"), 2,
               "devloop-install-issues: expected at most one directory argument, the project directory, got 2")

        # the listing
        fresh(); ans("issues", 1, "", "gh: Not logged in")
        expect("gh issue list does not answer: not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issues: not decided",
               "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "message: gh: Not logged in")
        fresh(); ans("issues", 0, "not json")
        expect("gh issue list prints no JSON list: not decided, 2", go(), 2, "issues: not decided")
        fresh(); issues()
        expect("no open issue: none carries the lines, 0", go(), 0,
               "issues: none of the 0 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries the lines install-tool and install-command")
        fresh(); issues((3, "A finding", "no lines here"), (4, "Install yq", "install-tool: yq\nno command line"))
        expect("issues without both lines: passed over, 0", go(), 0,
               "issues: none of the 2 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries the lines install-tool and install-command")
        called("issues without both lines: the blocking relation of none read",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body")

        # the tool standing: closed with the comment
        fresh(); issues((7, "Install devloop-selftest-present for task #5", body_present)); blocking(7, "open"); close(7)
        expect("the tool stands on the search path: closed, the task's issue, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 7", "kind: task", "title: Install devloop-selftest-present for task #5",
               "tool: " + present_line, "command: brew install devloop-selftest-present", "closed: yes")
        called("the tool stands: the listing, the blocking relation, the closing with the comment naming the line",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/7/dependencies/blocking",
               "issue close 7 --comment " + comment + present_line)
        # after an install typed in the session, --after-install: closed as at a start, the comment naming the install in the session and no start
        fresh(); issues((18, "Install devloop-selftest-present for task #5", body_present)); blocking(18, "open"); close(18)
        expect("--after-install, the tool standing: closed, 0", go("--after-install"), 0,
               "issue: 18", "kind: task", "tool: " + present_line, "closed: yes")
        called("--after-install: the closing with the comment naming an install typed in the session",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/18/dependencies/blocking",
               "issue close 18 --comment devloop closed this issue after an install typed in the session, the tool standing on this machine now: " + present_line)
        # the tool not standing: nothing done
        fresh(); issues((8, "Install devloop-selftest-absent", body_absent)); blocking(8)
        expect("the tool does not stand: not closed, the class's issue, the values trimmed, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 8", "kind: class",
               "tool: devloop-selftest-absent: not found on the search path (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "command: brew install devloop-selftest-absent", "closed: no")
        called("the tool does not stand: no closing called",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/8/dependencies/blocking")
        n[0] += 1
        if any(l.startswith("folder:") for l in got_out(go)):
            print("FAIL the tool does not stand and brew's folder is not read: a folder line stands there and should not"); red[0] += 1
        else:
            print("ok   the tool does not stand and brew's folder is not read: no folder line")
        # the tool installed by go into a folder the search path does not reach: not closed, the folder line
        fresh(); issues((17, "Install devloop-selftest-infolder for task #6", "install-tool: devloop-selftest-infolder\ninstall-command: go install example.invalid/x/cmd/devloop-selftest-infolder@latest\n")); blocking(17, "open")
        expect("the command installed into a folder the search path does not reach: not closed, the folder line, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 17", "kind: task",
               "tool: devloop-selftest-infolder: not found on the search path (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "command: go install example.invalid/x/cmd/devloop-selftest-infolder@latest", "closed: no")
        # the folder line read by its head and its tail: the folder stands between them as bash writes it on this system, ~/go/bin where the home directory is read as such
        n[0] += 1
        head = "folder: devloop-selftest-infolder: found at "
        tail = "go/bin/devloop-selftest-infolder, the folder go writes to, read off go env GOBIN (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1]
        if any(l.startswith(head) and l.endswith(tail) for l in got_out(go)):
            print("ok   the folder line names the folder go writes to, read off go env GOBIN")
        else:
            print("FAIL the folder line names the folder go writes to, read off go env GOBIN: no line from %r to %r" % (head, tail)); red[0] += 1
            print("    " + "\n    ".join(got_out(go)))
        called("the folder read: no closing called",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/17/dependencies/blocking")
        # a browser the program cannot read: not closed
        fresh(); issues((9, "Download WebKit", "install-tool: playwright:webkit\ninstall-command: npx playwright install webkit\n")); blocking(9, "closed", "open")
        expect("a browser not read: not closed, the cause, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 9", "kind: task",
               "tool: playwright:webkit: not read, because the project has no Playwright package to say which version of webkit it needs, no node_modules/.bin/playwright (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "closed: no")
        # a tool in no form the program reads
        fresh(); issues((10, "Install", "install-tool: a b\ninstall-command: x\n")); blocking(10)
        expect("a tool in no form the program reads: not read with its message, not closed, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 10", "tool: not read, because bin/devloop-tool-state refused the tool: devloop-tool-state: a TOOL is a bare word as it is called - letters, digits, dot, hyphen, plus and underscore - or VENDOR:NAME for a browser, got 'a b'",
               "closed: no")
        fresh(); issues((11, "Install", "install-tool: chrome:webkit\ninstall-command: x\n")); blocking(11)
        expect("a browser of a vendor the program refuses: not read with its message, not closed, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 11", "tool: not read, because bin/devloop-tool-state refused the tool: devloop-tool-state: a browser is named VENDOR:NAME with playwright, puppeteer or cypress as the vendor, and cypress:binary for Cypress, got 'chrome:webkit'",
               "closed: no")
        # several, in the order of the numbers, one closed and one not
        fresh(); issues((20, "Second", body_absent), (12, "First", body_present)); blocking(12, "open"); blocking(20, "open"); close(12)
        got = go()
        expect("several issues: in the order of the numbers, one closed, one not, 0", got, 0,
               "issue: 12", "closed: yes", "issue: 20", "closed: no")
        n[0] += 1
        out_lines = got[1].split("\n")
        if out_lines.index("issue: 12") < out_lines.index("issue: 20"):
            print("ok   several issues: the lower number first")
        else:
            print("FAIL several issues: the lower number first"); red[0] += 1
        # queries that do not answer
        fresh(); issues((13, "T", body_present)); ans("blocking.13", 1, "", "gh: HTTP 404")
        expect("the blocking relation does not answer: not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issues: not decided", "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/13/dependencies/blocking", "message: gh: HTTP 404")
        fresh(); issues((14, "T", body_present)); blocking(14, "open"); close(14, 1, "gh: HTTP 403")
        expect("the closing does not answer: the group with closed: no, then not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issue: 14", "closed: no", "issues: not decided", "command: gh issue close 14 --comment " + comment + present_line, "message: gh: HTTP 403")
        fresh(); issues((15, "First", body_present), (16, "Second", body_present)); blocking(15, "open"); close(15); ans("blocking.16", 1, "", "gh: HTTP 500")
        expect("a query fails after an issue was closed: that issue's group stands, then not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issue: 15", "closed: yes", "issues: not decided", "message: gh: HTTP 500", absent=("issue: 16",))

        # a command that does not answer within its limit, the limit set through LOADER: ended by the bound with the process it started, the message with the seconds of that limit, and the call back no later than those seconds and two after a call answered at once; the seconds longer on Windows, where the processes of a stand-in take longer to start
        short = 5 if os.name == "nt" else 3
        longer = 10 if os.name == "nt" else 3
        listing = "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body"
        # gh
        fresh(); ans("issues", 1, "", "gh: Not logged in")
        got, at_once = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "")
        expect("the limit of gh set to a few seconds and gh answering at once: as with sixty, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided", listing, "message: gh: Not logged in")
        open(os.path.join(S, "issues.hang"), "w").close()
        got, hung = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "")
        expect("gh does not answer within its limit: not decided, the command, no answer within the seconds, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided", listing, "message: no answer within %d seconds" % short)
        standing_still("gh does not answer within its limit: ended with the process it started, neither standing once the program has returned",
                       "marks.gh", "marks.started")
        in_time("gh does not answer within its limit: back no later than the limit and two seconds after a call answered at once", hung, at_once, short)
        # the tool state program under --build: its query a stand-in for the project's Playwright
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "p", "node_modules", ".bin"))
        playwright = os.path.join(T, "p", "node_modules", ".bin", "playwright")
        with open(playwright, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("#!/bin/sh\n" + HANG_SH % ("query", "query") + "echo 'no dry run scripted' >&2\nexit 1\n")
        os.chmod(playwright, os.stat(playwright).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        webkit = "install-tool: playwright:webkit\ninstall-command: npx playwright install webkit\n"
        fresh(); issues((21, "Download WebKit", webkit)); blocking(21)
        got, at_once = loaded("BUILD_SECONDS=%d" % longer, "")
        expect("the limit under --build set to a few seconds and the query answering at once: the line not read with the query's message, not closed, 0", got, 0,
               "issue: 21",
               "tool: playwright:webkit: not read, because the project's Playwright did not say which version of webkit it needs: node_modules/.bin/playwright install --dry-run webkit did not answer: no dry run scripted (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "closed: no")
        open(os.path.join(S, "query.hang"), "w").close()
        got, hung = loaded("BUILD_SECONDS=%d" % longer, "")
        expect("the tool state program does not answer within its limit under --build: not decided, the command, no answer within the seconds, the group not printed, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided", "command: bin/devloop-tool-state --build playwright:webkit",
               "message: no answer within %d seconds" % longer, absent=("issue: 21",))
        standing_still("the tool state program does not answer under --build: its bound, the query and the process the query started ended, none standing once the program has returned",
                       "marks.query", "marks.started")
        in_time("the tool state program does not answer under --build: back no later than the limit and two seconds after a call answered at once", hung, at_once, longer)
        # the same with the limit under --build as it stands, 72 seconds, and a few seconds of the whole call left: the self-test's clock at the ninety-five less those few, the fewest seconds a command starts with set to them, so that the tool state program has what is left and not its 72
        fresh(); issues((21, "Download WebKit", webkit)); blocking(21)
        open(os.path.join(S, "query.hang"), "w").close()
        got, hung = loaded("STEP=%d" % longer, "", clock=95 - longer)
        expect("the tool state program started with a few of the 95 seconds left has those and not its 72: not decided, the command, no answer within those seconds, the group not printed, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided", "command: bin/devloop-tool-state --build playwright:webkit",
               "message: no answer within %d seconds" % longer, absent=("issue: 21",))
        standing_still("the tool state program started with a few of the 95 seconds left: its bound, the query and the process the query started ended, none standing once the program has returned",
                       "marks.query", "marks.started")
        in_time("the tool state program started with a few of the 95 seconds left: back no later than those seconds and two after a call answered at once", hung, at_once, longer)
        shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(T, "p", "node_modules"))
        # the tool state program under --installed: its query the stand-in for go
        infolder = "install-tool: devloop-selftest-infolder\ninstall-command: go install example.invalid/x/cmd/devloop-selftest-infolder@latest\n"
        fresh(); issues((22, "Install devloop-selftest-infolder", infolder)); blocking(22)
        got, at_once = loaded("INSTALLED_SECONDS=%d" % longer, "")
        expect("the limit under --installed set to a few seconds and go answering at once: the group, not closed, 0", got, 0,
               "issue: 22", "kind: class", "closed: no")
        open(os.path.join(S, "go.hang"), "w").close()
        got, hung = loaded("INSTALLED_SECONDS=%d" % longer, "")
        expect("the tool state program does not answer within its limit under --installed: not decided, the command, no answer within the seconds, the group not printed, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided",
               "command: bin/devloop-tool-state --installed 'go install example.invalid/x/cmd/devloop-selftest-infolder@latest' devloop-selftest-infolder",
               "message: no answer within %d seconds" % longer, absent=("issue: 22",))
        standing_still("the tool state program does not answer under --installed: its bound, go and the process go started ended, none standing once the program has returned",
                       "marks.go", "marks.started")
        in_time("the tool state program does not answer under --installed: back no later than the limit and two seconds after a call answered at once", hung, at_once, longer)
        # no gh on PATH: nothing started, the system's message for a file that is not there
        fresh()
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "gh")
        expect("no gh on PATH: not decided, the command and the system's message naming gh, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided", listing, "message: %s" % FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "gh"))
        called("no gh on PATH: nothing started")
        # the limit over the whole call, on the self-test's clock: no command starts once fewer than ten of the ninety-five seconds are left, and a command that is not started answers as one that did not answer
        listed = "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body"
        fresh(); issues((15, "First", body_present), (16, "Second", body_present)); blocking(15, "open"); blocking(16, "open"); close(15); close(16)
        takes("issues", 50); takes("blocking.15", 20); takes("close.15", 16)
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=60", "", clock=0)
        expect("the list after 50 seconds, the first issue read and closed in 36 more, nine of the 95 left: the blocking relation of the second not started, the first group standing, then not decided with no time left, 2", got, 2,
               "issue: 15", "closed: yes", "issues: not decided",
               "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/16/dependencies/blocking",
               "message: no time left within the 95 seconds of this call", absent=("issue: 16",))
        called("nine of the 95 seconds left: the call of gh not started",
               listed, "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking",
               "issue close 15 --comment " + comment + present_line)
        fresh(); issues((15, "First", body_present)); blocking(15, "open"); close(15)
        takes("issues", 50); takes("blocking.15", 36)
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=60", "", clock=0)
        expect("the list after 50 seconds and the blocking relation after 36 more, nine of the 95 left: the tool state program not started, not decided with no time left, the group not printed, 2", got, 2,
               "issues: not decided", "command: bin/devloop-tool-state --build devloop-selftest-present",
               "message: no time left within the 95 seconds of this call", absent=("issue: 15",))
        called("nine of the 95 seconds left: the tool state program not started, nothing closed",
               listed, "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking")
        fresh(); issues((15, "First", body_present)); blocking(15, "open"); close(15)
        takes("issues", 50); takes("blocking.15", 35)
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=60", "", clock=0)
        expect("the list after 50 seconds and the blocking relation after 35 more, ten of the 95 left: the tool state program and the closing started with them, the issue closed, 0", got, 0,
               "issue: 15", "tool: " + present_line, "closed: yes")
        # no bash on PATH: under Windows, where bash runs the bound of every command, nothing is started and the message names bash; on macOS and Linux bash is looked up for the tool state program alone, so that gh is called and that program is not started, the message naming bash
        fresh(); issues((7, "Install devloop-selftest-present for task #5", body_present)); blocking(7, "open")
        got, _ = loaded("SECONDS=%d" % short, "bash")
        no_bash = "message: %s" % FileNotFoundError(errno.ENOENT, os.strerror(errno.ENOENT), "bash")
        under_windows = os.name == "nt"
        if under_windows:
            expect("no bash on PATH under Windows: not decided, the listing and the system's message naming bash, 2", got, 2,
                   "issues: not decided", listing, no_bash)
            called("no bash on PATH under Windows: nothing started")
        else:
            expect("no bash on PATH on macOS and Linux: not decided, the tool state program and the system's message naming bash, the group not printed, 2", got, 2,
                   "issues: not decided", "command: bin/devloop-tool-state --build devloop-selftest-present", no_bash, absent=("issue: 7",))
            called("no bash on PATH on macOS and Linux: the listing and the blocking relation called, the tool state program not started",
                   listed, "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/7/dependencies/blocking")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)
    if red[0]:
        print("SELF-TEST FAILED: %d of %d cases red" % (red[0], n[0]))
        return 1
    print("SELF-TEST PASSED: %d cases" % n[0])
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if argv == ["--self-test"]:
        return selftest()
    given = None
    dirs = 0
    after_install = False
    for a in argv:
        if a == "--after-install":
            after_install = True
            continue
        if a.startswith("-"):
            return usage("unknown option %s; the options are --after-install and --self-test" % a)
        dirs += 1
        given = a
    if dirs > 1:
        return usage("expected at most one directory argument, the project directory, got %d" % dirs)
    return read(project_dir(given), after_install)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
